---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { SITE_NAME, UMBRELLA_NAME } from "../lib/site";
---
<Base title="Disclaimer" description={`What ${SITE_NAME} is for, what it is not for, and the limits of the results it produces.`}>
  <h1 class="text-[26px] font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-[32px]">Disclaimer</h1>
  <section class="prose-doc mt-6 max-w-3xl text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
    <h2>These results do not replace professional work</h2>
    <p>
      {SITE_NAME} produces indicative figures for orientation and cross-checking. They
      <strong> do not replace</strong> an accredited calibration, a shielding design, a dose
      assessment, a radiation survey, or the judgement of a qualified radiation protection
      professional. Decisions affecting the safety of people must rest on measurements and on
      assessments made by someone competent and accountable for them.
    </p>

    <h2>Every result carries assumptions</h2>
    <p>
      Each calculator states the geometry and the physical assumptions it applies. Those assumptions
      are simplifications of real situations. A point-source dose rate does not describe an extended
      source. Attenuation without buildup underestimates. An empirical range formula carries about
      ten percent uncertainty. Where an assumption dominates the answer, the calculator says so on
      the page rather than in a footnote.
    </p>

    <h2>Data and accuracy</h2>
    <p>
      Nuclear and attenuation data come from IAEA and NIST as described under
      <a href="/calc/methods/" class="underline">Methods and data</a>. Evaluated data are revised
      over time and different compilations differ. The computed values are tested against published
      figures and agree within the tolerances stated there, but no warranty of accuracy, fitness for
      any purpose, or freedom from error is given.
    </p>

    <h2>No relationship with standards bodies</h2>
    <p>
      {SITE_NAME} and {UMBRELLA_NAME} are independent. They are not affiliated with, endorsed by, or
      accredited by the IAEA, NIST, the IEC, the ISO, or any regulatory authority. References to
      published standards are made for identification only, and no text or table from any standard is
      reproduced here.
    </p>

    <h2>Liability</h2>
    <p>
      The site is provided as is. To the fullest extent permitted by law, no liability is accepted
      for any loss or damage arising from the use of, or reliance on, anything produced by it.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
